ated to the application of statistical methods and experimental designs aimed at enhancing quality control and improvement within engineering disciplines. The journal serves as a platform for researchers and practitioners to disseminate findings that contribute to the advancement of quality engineering practices.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nWhile the journal itself does not have a specific mathematical formulation, it encompasses a variety of statistical models and methodologies relevant to quality engineering, including but not limited to control charts, process capability analysis, and design of experiments (DOE). These models are essential for analyzing and improving quality in engineering processes.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nQuality Engineering publishes articles that may include various experimental designs such as factorial designs, response surface methodologies, and Taguchi methods. Each of these designs is structured to systematically investigate the effects of multiple factors on quality outcomes, allowing for robust conclusions to be drawn from experimental data.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of the statistical methods and experimental designs discussed in Quality Engineering relies on several assumptions, including:\n- Independence of observations\n- Normality of data distribution\n- Homogeneity of variance (equal variances across groups)\n- Adequate sample size to ensure statistical power\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nQuality Engineering is applicable in scenarios where statistical methods can be employed to solve quality-related problems in engineering. Selection criteria for methodologies published in the journal often include the complexity of the problem, the number of factors involved, and the desired precision of the results.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis procedures highlighted in Quality Engineering typically involve statistical tests such as 